From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] QFileSystemEngine: fall back when renameat2 is ENOSYS

QFileSystemEngine::renameFile() tries renameat2(RENAME_NOREPLACE) first and
only continues to its link()+unlink() fallback when that fails with EINVAL,
which is what some non-local filesystems return. Any other errno is
reported to the caller as the result of the rename.

When Qt is built against headers that provide renameat2() but runs on a
kernel that does not implement the syscall, the call fails with ENOSYS, and
so does every QFile::rename(), QDir::rename() and anything built on them.
The LuneOS armv7 Halium machines (tenderloin-halium, hammerhead-halium)
run Linux 3.4, which predates renameat2() (3.15), so a rename could never
succeed there.

Treat ENOSYS like EINVAL and fall through to the link()+unlink() path that
is already there for filesystems without renameat2() support. That path
keeps the no-replace semantics, so behaviour is unchanged on kernels that
have the syscall.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/src/corelib/io/qfilesystemengine_unix.cpp
+++ b/src/corelib/io/qfilesystemengine_unix.cpp
@@ -1764,8 +1764,10 @@
     if (renameat2(AT_FDCWD, srcPath, AT_FDCWD, tgtPath, RENAME_NOREPLACE) == 0)
         return true;
 
-    // We can also get EINVAL for some non-local filesystems.
-    if (errno != EINVAL) {
+    // We can also get EINVAL for some non-local filesystems, and ENOSYS from a
+    // kernel older than the one the headers were built against (renameat2()
+    // is Linux 3.15).
+    if (errno != EINVAL && errno != ENOSYS) {
         error = QSystemError(errno, QSystemError::StandardLibraryError);
         return false;
     }
